● Q20 is recorded as DEC-068 (commit e05f8fe). Now Q19 from first principles.

  What a confidence interval promises. Suppose the true effect of the learned reader over a control, on the whole population of facts we could have drawn, is some number Δ. We never see Δ; we see an estimate from the facts we did draw. A 95 % confidence interval is a procedure that, if you repeated the whole experiment many
  times with fresh draws, would contain Δ in 95 % of the repetitions. That property is called coverage, and it is the entire content of the label "95 %". A procedure whose intervals contain the truth 75 % of the time is not a 95 % interval no matter what it is called.

  What our data are. Each dataset gives three realizations: three independent draws of 1,000 facts (300 for MQuAKE) from the cleared register, made with your seed. Within a realization we run five edit orders, and each cell scores hundreds of items. Those inner numbers are plentiful, but they are not independent samples of Δ:
  they all share the same draw of facts, and the items inside a cell share one memory state. The registered analysis (DEC-057) therefore treats the realization as the unit of replication, which is correct, and that leaves three independent numbers per contrast: the three realization means.

  What the bootstrap then does. The percentile bootstrap estimates uncertainty by resampling the data with replacement and asking how much the mean moves. With three values, say −0.02, 0.03 and 0.12, there are only 27 possible resamples. The resampled mean can never be lower than −0.02 (only when the smallest value is drawn
  all three times, probability 1/27 ≈ 3.7 %) or higher than 0.12. A 97.5 % interval cuts off 1.25 % in each tail, and the Bonferroni-adjusted one cuts off 0.04 %; both cuts fall inside those 3.7 % end atoms, so both intervals come out as [−0.02, 0.12], the observed minimum and maximum. Ten thousand draws instead of a thousand
  change nothing; the arithmetic is exhausted at 27.

  Why that has 75 % coverage. Take the simplest sampling model: each realization mean equals Δ plus an error that is equally likely to be positive or negative. The interval from the smallest to the largest of three values misses Δ exactly when all three errors have the same sign: probability 2 × (1/2)³ = 25 %. So the procedure
  covers the truth 75 % of the time, whatever the label says. Codex reproduced 75.1 % in a 200,000-trial simulation. Bonferroni cannot help, because dividing α only asks the interval to be wider, and it is already as wide as three points allow. A permutation or sign-flip test has the same wall from the other side: with three
  exchangeable signs the smallest attainable two-sided p-value is 2/8 = 0.25.

  Why the inner numbers cannot rescue it. The hundreds of items in a cell make each realization mean precise; they say nothing about how much a fresh draw of facts would change that mean. The between-realization spread is the only evidence we have on that, and with three draws it is estimated from two degrees of freedom. A
  Student-t interval that uses it honestly multiplies the observed spread by 4.3, giving very wide intervals under an explicit normality assumption. That is option C's secondary display: not a fix, but a calibrated statement under a stated assumption.

  What would actually fix it, and what it costs. Independent realizations. Ten would give the bootstrap something to work with; each costs 1,350 cleared subjects and about 95 cells, so even a fourth (option B) is the most the register allows for zsRE and CounterFact, none for MQuAKE, and four draws only lift the range's
  coverage to about 87.5 %. The design was sized for effect margins (a 0.05 retention gain), not for calibrated inference across draws, and that is what it can deliver: replicated effect sizes.

  What we can honestly say (option A). For every contrast: the effect in each of the three realizations, the spread across orders inside each, and whether the direction and rough size replicate. The registered classifier labels stay, computed exactly as registered, but described as preliminary decision summaries. No claim of
  demonstrated 95 % familywise control or of an established population-level effect. That is weaker than the original protocol implied and stronger than most single-draw editing results in the literature, which report one draw with no replication at all.

  Two wording consequences Codex also flagged: "positive" requires a point gain of at least 0.05 with a lower bound above zero, not a lower bound of 0.05; "negative" can mean missing the 0.05 target while a smaller real gain exists. Effects should be published before category names.

  One more option, for completeness: a hierarchical model with items nested in realizations would use both levels of variation. It gives more power to the within-realization comparison, but the between-realization variance is still estimated from three groups, so the population-level claim does not improve. I would not adopt
  it now; it can be an additive analysis later.

  My recommendation stands: A, with C's t-interval displayed beside it. Your answer goes straight into protocol D.5 within the re-bind.

