❯ Does this relate to the heavy-tailed issue that is referenced in my abstract which is the ostensible focus of all this work?                                                                                                                                                                                                          

● Only at the edges. They are two different problems, and it is worth keeping them apart in the talk.
  
  Q19 is a small-number problem, not a tail problem. It would arise with perfectly Gaussian data: three independent draws of the fact population give three numbers, and no procedure can turn three numbers into a calibrated 95 % statement about the population of draws. Nothing about heavy tails causes it, and it would not go
  away if every distribution in the study were thin-tailed.

  Where the two touch, in three places:

  - Which endpoints suffer most. The retention and success rates are bounded proportions averaged over 1,000 items per cell; their realization means are well behaved, so the three-cluster limitation is "only" the coverage problem. The tail endpoints, the maximum harm, ES99, the exceedance counts, are dominated by rare
    positions, so their value in a realization depends on whether that draw happened to contain the few facts that provoke the large collisions. Across draws those statistics fluctuate far more than proportions do, and with three draws the caution is correspondingly stronger. That is the heavy-tailed theme biting the
    inference.
  - What is robust anyway. The abstract's central claim is within-population: averages hide harm that is concentrated in a small number of contexts. That is established inside each cell over 245,237 positions, and it recurred in all four development cells (half the KL in 64 to 171 positions). Replication across three fresh
    draws will show whether the concentration recurs; it does not need a calibrated interval to be a real finding. The talk's structural point survives Q19 intact.
  - Mean thresholds versus tail summaries. The fidelity result is the theme in miniature: the registered limit is a mean KL of 0.001, the model measures 0.0055, and 99.6 % of positions are untouched. A mean threshold is the wrong instrument for a concentrated quantity, which is exactly what "thriving in the extremes" argues,
    and why the tail statistics and the concentration shares now sit beside every mean in the reports.

  So the honest framing for October: the heavy-tail claim (harm is concentrated, and summaries built on means misdescribe it) is measured and replicated; the population-level inference about effect sizes is preliminary because three independent draws are all the register allowed. The first is the abstract's subject; the second
  is a limitation of any editing study of this size, stated plainly.

